TITLE:: SDGWindow
summary:: the control window of a SuperDirtGUI: orbit strips, global effects, the sound explorer, what was loaded
categories:: Libraries>SuperDirtGUI
related:: Classes/SuperDirtGUI, Classes/SDGSampleEditor, Classes/DirtOrbit, Classes/GlobalDirtEffect

DESCRIPTION::
code::~sdg.gui:: makes one, after code::~dirt.start::. One strip per orbit on
the left; three tabs on the right; a hint bar along the bottom that says what
the pointer is over.

code::
~sdg.gui;                  // or SDGWindow(~sdg)
~sdg.gui.selectTab(1);     // 0 effects, 1 sound explorer, 2 loaded
~sdg.gui.search = "808";   // narrow the explorer
~sdg.gui.close;
::

subsection:: Sessions

Along the top: a menu of the saved sessions, strong::load session:: for the one it
shows, strong::save session:: to keep the whole mixer under a name, asked for in a
small window that warns before it replaces one. Choosing in the menu changes nothing
until load session is pressed. See link::Classes/SDGSession::.

subsection:: The strips

One per orbit, named after it: code::orbit 0:: is where Tidal's code::d1::
plays, code::orbit 1:: code::d2::, and so on. The name selects the orbit for
the effects tab and the explorer's play button.

Under the name, the orbit's inserts, top first - the order the signal takes
(link::Classes/SDGInsert::). A click opens an insert's window - a VST plugin's
own editor - a right click bypasses, moves or removes it (and, for a VST
plugin, shows its parameters as sliders or remembers its state now), and +
insert adds an eq, a compressor, an insight or a VST plugin after the others,
opening its window. A VST plugin is picked in link::#-vstDialog:: first. A
bypassed insert is greyed and in brackets.

Then peak and rms meters, one per channel, reading after the inserts and the
fader - SuperDirt's own code::dirt_rms::, 20 replies a second while the window
is open (link::Classes/SuperDirtGUI#-startMeters::), a tick every 5 dB from
-60 to 0 - and beside them the fader, its level in dB under it
(link::#*faderString::), then the highest peak since you last clicked it, in dB,
red once it reached 0. Mute and solo do not change the level shown. The fader,
mute and solo are
link::Classes/SuperDirtGUI#-setFader::, link::Classes/SuperDirtGUI#-setMute::
and link::Classes/SuperDirtGUI#-setSolo::, so the state lives in the loader
and the strips follow its code::\mixer:: notification; the monitor's limiter
mode; and the orbit's output bus, as a number, and as a dropdown of BusManager
names when that quark is installed.

Above the strips, a button opens the BusManager window and another the
BusRecorder window, through link::Classes/SuperDirtGUI#-recorder::. Each is
disabled when its quark is not installed.

subsection:: The effects tab

The global effects of the selected orbit as tracks (link::Classes/SDGTrack::),
a strip each, left to right in the order of the chain. From the top: the name,
which shows the effect's parameters under the strips; load effect..., another
global effect on the same track, in place of this one, its inserts and fader
staying; off pauses the synth and on resumes it; the arrows move the track, the
cross takes it and its inserts out; + pre, the inserts before the effect, a
click for an insert's window and a right click for bypass, move and remove; the
effect's name; + post, the inserts after it; meters and a fader on what the track
adds to the orbit, the dry signal untouched. Under the strips, add puts any other
SynthDef that follows the global-effect convention (code::dryBus::,
code::effectBus::, code::gate::) on a new track, and knobs, dragged up and down,
set the selected effect's parameters. A value set here is written into the orbit's
code::defaultParentEvent:: and pushed to the running synth, so the next event
from Tidal carries the same value and nothing flips back; the code::x:: puts a
parameter back to what it was before the window touched it. Knobs use
link::#*paramSpecs:: when there is one for the name and the SynthDef default
otherwise.

Under them, the same orbit's inserts: a row each with on (bypass), the name
(its window), the arrows (its place among the inserts) and the cross, and a
menu to add one. The kinds whose extension is not installed are listed as
such and cannot be added.

subsection:: The sound explorer

The sound library, not the disk: a folder per bank that opens onto its
samples, the synth events in a folder of their own, a search over both. It is
a list with columns, as in the Finder's list view (code::SDGExplorerView::
draws it): name, duration, bpm, key and tags, and code::n::, date added and
date created a right click on the header away. A click on a header sorts by
it, a second click the other way (link::#-sortBy::); banks among banks, each
bank's samples within it. The bpm, the key and the tags come from
link::Classes/SDGSampleTags::, which the window asks to analyse the library
when it opens. The search takes words for names and code::tag:kick::,
code::-tag:melodic::, code::bpm:120:: (its halves and doubles too) and
code::key:am:: (link::#*parseSearch::); the buttons under it are the commonest
tags, alt-click to leave one out. code::t:: edits the selected sample's tags,
or adds tags to every sample of a selected bank (link::#-editTagsDialog::). Enter
or a double click plays the selected sound into the orbit chosen below through
the code::\dirt:: event type, with the speed, amp and pan knobs; code::e:: or
edit... opens a sample in link::Classes/SDGSampleEditor::; a right click shows
the file where it lives, opens it, or copies its path. The code::n:: box is
what plays: picking a sample sets it, editing it picks a sibling. load folder...
is link::#-loadFolder:: behind a dialog. The window
follows the loader's code::\samples:: and code::\reloaded:: notifications and,
for banks loaded straight through code::~dirt.loadSoundFiles::, compares the
library's size once a second.

subsection:: The modules tab

What the four folders gave - synths, effects (the folder code::modules/::),
players, global effects - a list each, and under each list new... and edit...; then the reload and free buttons,
and load folder..., the same button as in the sound explorer.

new... asks for a name and which file to copy - the one selected in the list,
else the folder's example - and says as you type whether the name will do
(link::Classes/SuperDirtGUI#-nameProblem::). make is link::#-makeFile:: - the
copy is written, loaded, selected in its list, and opened. edit..., a double
click or enter opens the file the selected name came from, through
link::#*openCode::, which in the IDE makes it a document whose code::~dirt::
and code::~sdg:: are the loader's. A right click on a name offers the same, shows
the file in the Finder, or copies its path. The line under the lists says which
file the selected name came from.

At the bottom stands what the selected name does: the description and the
parameters with their ranges, as link::Classes/SuperDirtGUI#-notes:: reads them
from the comments at the top of its file. They are read again when the file is
saved. A file without a code::// parameters:: section shows the controls of its
SynthDef, with their defaults.

While the window is open it reads a content file again within a second of its
being saved - link::Classes/SuperDirtGUI#-reloadChangedFiles:: on the same
once-a-second poll as the library - so editing is: edit, save, hear it. A file
whose last read failed is marked in its list, and the version that loaded last
plays on; one that failed before it defined anything is listed by its file
name. link::#*reloadOnSave:: turns the reading off.


CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: faderString
A fader's level as a strip writes it under the fader, to 0.1 dB.
ARGUMENT:: amp
the level, linear.
RETURNS:: a String: code::"-1.0 dB"::, code::"+2.5 dB"::, code::"0.0 dB"::, code::"-inf dB"::.

METHOD:: new
ARGUMENT:: sdg
the link::Classes/SuperDirtGUI::; code::SuperDirtGUI.current:: when nil. Its
SuperDirt must have orbits.
RETURNS:: an SDGWindow, already open.

METHOD:: hiddenEffects
The orbit effects the window never lists or moves: SuperDirt's
code::dirt_rms:: and code::dirt_monitor::, and the loader's fader.

METHOD:: isHiddenEffect
Whether the global effects list leaves an effect out: one of
link::#*hiddenEffects::, or an insert, which has a list of its own.
ARGUMENT:: name
the effect's name.

METHOD:: insertColour
The colour of an insert's button, by kind.
ARGUMENT:: kind
code::\eq::, code::\compressor:: or code::\insight::.

METHOD:: insertKindName
What a menu calls a kind of insert, and whether its extension is installed.
ARGUMENT:: kind
code::\eq::, code::\compressor:: or code::\insight::.

METHOD:: hiddenParams
SynthDef controls that are plumbing, never offered as parameters.

METHOD:: paramSpecs
An IdentityDictionary from parameter name to link::Classes/ControlSpec::, for
the knobs. Holds SuperDirt's delay, reverb, leslie and limiter parameters
and the templates' own; add yours.

METHOD:: effectCandidates
Every SynthDef for the loader's channel count that follows the global-effect
convention, is not hidden, and is not in the orbit yet.

METHOD:: explorerTab
The tab a new window opens on; 1, the explorer, by default.

METHOD:: reloadOnSave
Whether an open window reads content files again when they change on disk. true
by default; set it false to read them only with reload or
link::Classes/SuperDirtGUI#-loadFile::.

METHOD:: codeEditor
A Function of a path and the loader that opens a content file, for an editor
other than the IDE; nil by default, and then link::#*openCode:: decides.
code::
// Visual Studio Code, if its command line tool is installed
SDGWindow.codeEditor = { |path| ("code" + path.shellQuote).unixCmd };
SDGWindow.codeEditor = nil;   // the IDE again
::

METHOD:: openCode
Open a content file. Inside the SuperCollider IDE, as a link::Classes/Document::
whose environment holds the loader's code::~dirt:: and code::~sdg::, with the
IDE's own environment behind them, so a block evaluated in it sees what a load
sees; a document that is already open is brought to the front as it is.
Outside the IDE, with the system's app for the file. link::#*codeEditor::, when
set, is asked instead. Saving is what puts the change into SuperDirt (see the
modules tab above): evaluating a global effect's block in the document adds its
SynthDef, but the running effect starts again only when the file is read.
ARGUMENT:: path
the file.
ARGUMENT:: sdg
the link::Classes/SuperDirtGUI::.
RETURNS:: true when something was asked to open it.

METHOD:: colBg
METHOD:: colPanel
METHOD:: colText
METHOD:: colDim
METHOD:: colAccent
METHOD:: colOn
METHOD:: colWarn
METHOD:: colWave
METHOD:: colWaveDim
